Warm up Defender scan during Windows install

Windows Defender scans a binary on its first execution and caches the
verdict per file instance. A freshly installed rnote.exe therefore costs
roughly 4.5 s extra on the user's first double-click, on top of the ~2 s
steady-state startup, and is fast every launch after that.

Measured on this machine: 6.8 s first launch against 2.3 s afterwards. A
hardlink to an already-executed file shows no penalty, a byte-identical
fresh copy shows the full one, and an on-demand Start-MpScan does not
satisfy it -- only real execution does. Stripping debug symbols from
218 MB to 21 MB changed nothing, so image size is not the driver.

Run the binary once, hidden, as an install step so that scan lands inside
the progress bar where waiting is expected. This relocates the cost, it
does not remove it; the root fix is code signing, which builds the
reputation that makes the lookup cheap.

